window.MARDENHOLT_PUBLISHED_CHAPTERS = [{"title":"Chapter I — The First Road","date":"2026-11-04","zone":"Elwynn Forest","companions":"Spagetoll Cuh, Thundrus Buccaklapper, Quillb Picklepocket","quests":"From the peaceful valley of Northshire to the forests of Elwynn, our swords found no shortage of work. We hunted Hogger and his gnoll brethren, ventured into Stormwind, and even found time for a little fishing.","gear":"Our company acquired new equipment throughout the day. I also spent time at the forge, fashioning useful gear for myself and Thundrus. Mining, cooking, fishing, and the tending of wounds have likewise begun to occupy my attention.","story":"Day One — Northshire, Elwynn Forest\n\nWhat a day it has been.\n\nThis morning, I set forth in Northshire, uncertain of what the road might hold. I had scarcely begun my journey when fortune brought three fellow adventurers into my company.\n\nThe first was Spagetoll Cuh, followed by Thundrus Buccaklapper and Quillb Picklepocket.\n\nThough we had only just met, we shared a common purpose: to lend our strength to the defence of mankind and those who cannot defend themselves.\n\nIt seems the Light saw fit to place the four of us upon the same road.\n\nTogether, we undertook whatever duties Northshire had to offer. There was much to be done, and little time for idle hands. Yet I found the work easier in good company, and before long we had left the valley behind us.\n\nOur road led us to Goldshire, where we decided that a little rest was well deserved.\n\nAnd by the Light, what a lively place it proved to be!\n\nThere was ale, laughter, and more merriment than I had expected to find in such a modest village. For a while, swords and duties were forgotten, and I enjoyed the simple pleasure of good company.\n\nAlas, Quillb Picklepocket was obliged to take his leave.\n\nOur fellowship of four was reduced to three, though I trust our paths shall cross again before long.\n\nWe did not remain idle.\n\nThe forests of Elwynn are troubled by gnolls, and word of a particularly fearsome brute named Hogger reached our ears. Spagetoll, Thundrus, and I took up the hunt, determined to rid the countryside of these troublesome creatures.\n\nNot every danger wears the colours of an enemy army. Some lurk within the very forests that ordinary folk call home.\n\nOur travels also brought us to the magnificent city of Stormwind. Its towering walls and proud buildings are a sight to behold. Though my heart remains with Lordaeron, I cannot deny the grandeur of this southern kingdom.\n\nI even found occasion to cast a fishing line into the waters of the city. A peculiar pastime for a wandering knight, perhaps, but I found the quiet rather agreeable.\n\nThere is a certain peace in watching the water and waiting for a fish that may or may not possess the courtesy to bite.\n\nOur duties carried us farther through Elwynn, toward Stone Cairn Lake and the Eastvale Logging Camp, where more work awaited us.\n\nYet I have discovered that there is more to adventuring than the sword.\n\nToday I devoted considerable effort to learning the blacksmith's craft. The heat of the forge and the ringing of hammer upon iron proved strangely satisfying. I managed to fashion useful pieces of equipment for myself and for Thundrus.\n\nI confess to feeling rather proud of the work.\n\nI have also taken up mining, fishing, first aid, and cooking. A knight who can mend his own equipment, tend his wounds, and prepare a decent meal is surely better equipped for the road ahead.\n\nThough I suspect my cooking shall require rather more practice than my swordsmanship.\n\nNow, as I put these words to parchment, I find myself weary but content.\n\nThis morning, I began my journey with little knowledge of what awaited me. Tonight, I have companions, new skills, and a growing understanding of the world beyond the lands of my birth.\n\nI cannot say where the road shall lead us tomorrow, nor what adventures await Spagetoll, Thundrus, Quillb, and me.\n\nBut I find myself eager to discover it.\n\nMay the Light watch over our fellowship.\n\nSer Atte Mardenholt\n\nThe Oath Outlives the Crown.","outcome":"Tomorrow, I intend to set forth for Westfall. \nWhat awaits me beyond the borders of Elwynn, I cannot yet say.\n\nBut I trust the road will provide its share of adventure.","photos":[{"src":"assets/chapter-01-plate-01.webp","caption":" Goldshire"},{"src":"assets/chapter-01-plate-02.webp","caption":"Fishing in Stormwind"},{"src":"assets/chapter-01-plate-03.webp","caption":"Sumtingwong, Wuilby, Atte, Thundrus"}]},{"title":"Chapter II — Beneath the Mountain","date":"2026-11-05","zone":"Westfall and Ironforge","companions":"Furrassic Maul","quests":"Westfall duties and two expeditions through the Hall of Thanes","gear":"Several valuable equipment upgrades","story":"Day Two — Westfall, Ironforge & the Hall of Thanes\n\nBy the Light, my feet have scarcely known a moment's rest this day.\n\nAs I had intended, I made my way into Westfall, leaving the green forests of Elwynn behind for the golden fields and windswept farmlands of the west.\n\nI had expected a peaceful countryside. Instead, I found a land whose people have known far too much hardship.\n\nThe farms are troubled, the roads are dangerous, and the Defias Brotherhood seems to have spread its influence throughout the region.\n\nI spent much of the day travelling alone, lending my sword wherever it was needed.\n\nI have now completed the work available to me in Westfall, at least for the present. \nYet I cannot shake the feeling that the greatest threat to these people remains unanswered.\n\nThe Defias Brotherhood.\n\nBut my adventures were not confined to Westfall.\n\nToday I also had the pleasure of travelling alongside Furrassic Maul, a Night Elf druid whose company I have come to appreciate.\n\nOur journey carried us north into the mountains of Dun Morogh, where I beheld the great dwarven city of Ironforge for the very first time.\n\nWhat a magnificent sight!\n\nThe very mountain seems to have been hollowed out and filled with fire. Great furnaces roar beneath the stone, and the ringing of hammers echoes through its immense halls.\n\nBeing a blacksmith myself, though still a humble apprentice to the craft, I could scarcely help but admire the workmanship of the dwarves.\n\nYet we had not travelled so far merely to admire their city.\n\nDeep beneath Ironforge lies an ancient burial place known as the Hall of Thanes, where the honoured rulers of ages past have been laid to rest.\n\nUnfortunately, those sacred halls have not remained undisturbed.\n\nFurrassic and I ventured into their depths alongside other adventurers, facing the dangers that had taken hold of the ancient tombs.\n\nAnd once was apparently not enough.\nWe completed the Hall of Thanes twice!\n\nBy the end of our second expedition, I was thoroughly exhausted. \nMy armour felt twice its usual weight, and even raising my sword had become an effort.\n\nI suspect Furrassic was little better off.\n\nStill, there was satisfaction in knowing we had endured, and the rewards were most welcome. \nI acquired several fine pieces of equipment throughout the day, leaving me better prepared for whatever dangers lie ahead.\n\nThere was another noteworthy development today.\n\nOur growing fellowship has taken a name of its own:\n\nPlot Armor Crew.\n\nAmong its members are Quillb Picklepocket, Thundrus Buccaklapper, Spagetoll Cuh, Furrassic Maul, and myself.\n\nI am told there are others among our ranks whom I have yet to meet. I look forward to making their acquaintance.\n\nIt is a rather curious name for a company of adventurers, I must admit. \nThough if this so called Plot Armor Crew can spare us a few broken bones, I shall not complain.\n\nAnd now, at last, I have returned to Sentinel Hill.\n\nThe hour grows late, and I am grateful for the opportunity to rest.\n\nWestfall has taught me much today. Its people are weary, yet they endure. \nTheir hardships remind me why I first took up the sword, and why the oath of House Mardenholt must remain more than words upon a banner.\n\nTomorrow, I intend to turn my attention once more to the Defias Brotherhood.\n\nThere are preparations to be made, supplies to gather, and dangers beneath the Deadmines that cannot be ignored.\n\nIf the rumours are true, there is more to this brotherhood than a collection of common thieves.\n\nI mean to discover just how deep their wickedness runs.\n\nBut that is a matter for tomorrow.\n\nFor tonight, I shall enjoy a warm meal, find a comfortable bed, and give these weary legs the rest they have earned.\n\nMay the Light watch over those who still walk the roads tonight.\n\nSer Atte Mardenholt\n\nThe Oath Outlives the Crown.","outcome":"Prepare to confront the Defias Brotherhood and venture into the Deadmines.","photos":[{"src":"assets/chapter-02-plate-01.webp","caption":"Hall of Thanes "},{"src":"assets/chapter-02-plate-02.webp","caption":"Plot Armor Crew"}]},{"title":"Chapter III — Of Fallen Kings and Red Hills","date":"2026-11-06","zone":"Westfall · Deadmines · Dalaran · Ruins of Lordaeron · Redridge Mountains · Duskwood · Lakeshire Inn","companions":"Furrassic Maul, Lilbigmenace Thornwhisper","quests":"Completed the Deadmines twice with Furrassic Maul, defeating Edwin VanCleef both times.\n\nVisited Dalaran and explored its magnificent city.\n\nEntered and completed the Ruins of Lordaeron dungeon.\n\nCompleted the quests undertaken throughout Redridge Mountains, including fighting Blackrock orcs.\n\nMet guildmate Civa Warrior and Lilbigmenace Thornwhisper and her hunter pet, Milfhunter. \n\nFished together at Lake Everstill.\n\nBegan questing in Duskwood alongside Furrassic.\n\nReunited with Quillb Picklepocket and Thundrus Buccaklapper\n\nAcquired improved equipment.","gear":"New gear","story":"Day Three —When I awoke at Sentinel Hill, I thought my business in Westfall was nearing its end. \nThe Defias Brotherhood had troubled those lands long enough, and there remained one particularly troublesome man whose name had been spoken far too often: Edwin VanCleef.\n\nFortunately, I was not to face him alone.\n\nFurrassic Maul joined me once more, and together we ventured into the Deadmines, beneath the troubled hills of Westfall. \nThere, among the Brotherhood's hidden works, we fought our way to its master and brought Edwin VanCleef down.\n\nTwice, in fact.\n\nI should think defeating a man once ought to settle the matter, but apparently the Defias are a stubborn lot. \nStill, two victories are better than one, and we emerged with finer equipment and considerably less patience for masked brigands.\n\nYet the day was far from finished.\n\nMy travels carried me north, where I beheld the magnificent city of Dalaran. Its towering spires and strange splendour were unlike anything I had yet seen upon the road. \nFor a little while, I was content simply to stand and admire it.\n\nBut there was another place I wished to see, one far closer to my heart.\n\nLordaeron.\n\nI entered the Ruins of Lordaeron, the shattered remains of the kingdom whose name I still bear with pride.\n\nIt is a strange thing to stand among the ruins of one's homeland. To remember that these broken stones were once part of a kingdom that men believed would endure. I ventured through the dangers of the ruins and completed my undertaking there, yet it was the place itself that remained with me afterward.\n\nI have sworn that the oath of House Mardenholt shall outlive the crown.\n\nToday, among those fallen stones, I was reminded why.\n\nFrom there my road eventually turned toward the red hills of the east.\n\nRedridge Mountains proved a land of both beauty and considerable trouble. The waters of Lake Everstill lay beneath the great red mountains, while the people of Lakeshire had no shortage of work for a wandering swordsman.\n\nFurrassic and I spent much of our time answering the calls for aid throughout the region. By the time our business was concluded, I had worked through the quests Redridge had placed before me.\n\nAmong our encounters was another member of the Plot Armor Crew: Lilbigmenace Thornwhisper, a huntress accompanied by a pet bearing the rather extraordinary name Milfhunter.\n\nI confess, there are certain names that must make an innkeeper hesitate when filling out the guest ledger.\n\nThe three of us joined forces to deal with Blackrock orcs troubling the region. Yet not every moment was spent with weapons drawn. We also found time to cast our fishing lines into Lake Everstill.\n\nI stood upon a tree stump beside the water, rod in hand, with Lilbigmenace fishing nearby. After so many battles, there was something rather pleasant about doing nothing more dangerous than waiting for a fish to bite.\n\nOur adventures then led Furrassic and me into Duskwood.\n\nWhat a change from the bright hills of Redridge.\n\nThe forest was shrouded in darkness, with old trees and great webs hanging above the roads. I carried a torch through the gloom while Furrassic travelled beside me.\n\nWe completed some of the work that awaited us there, though Duskwood is certainly a place that deserves further exploration.\n\nAs the day drew toward its end, I returned to Redridge, where fortune granted me yet another welcome meeting.\n\nThere I encountered Quillb Picklepocket and Thundrus Buccaklapper, familiar faces whose company had already brightened the earliest days of my travels. Alongside them stood Civa Warrior, a new companion whom I was pleased to meet.\n\nWe gathered beneath the night sky, fellow adventurers of the Plot Armor Crew, each having travelled roads of their own.\n\nThere is comfort in discovering that, wherever the road may lead, familiar faces have a habit of appearing when they are most welcome.\n\nAnd so I have finally found myself a room at Lakeshire Inn.\n\nMy equipment is improved, my travels have carried me from the depths of Westfall to the ruins of my homeland, and I have made yet another friend along the way.\n\nThree days upon the road, and already I have seen more of Azeroth than I once imagined.\n\nMy feet ache, my armour requires attention, and I fear I shall be asleep before this ink is dry.\n\nA worthy day, all the same.\n\nThe oath outlives the crown.\n\n— Ser Atte Mardenholt","outcome":"Two victories over Edwin VanCleef, the completion of the Ruins of Lordaeron, a great deal of work accomplished in Redridge, and the beginnings of another adventure in Duskwood.","photos":[{"src":"assets/chapter-03-plate-01.webp","caption":"Dalaran"},{"src":"assets/chapter-03-plate-02.webp","caption":"Duskwood"}]},{"title":"Chapter IV — The Long Road to Booty Bay","date":"2026-11-07","zone":"Duskwood · Silverpine Forest · Ironforge · Stormwind · Wetlands · Westfall · Booty Bay","companions":"Lilbigmenace Thornwhisper, Furrassic Maul, Civa Warrior, Bu Shwacker,  Fat Dwarfs","quests":"Questing in Duskwood\nProfessions improvements \nHelping out a guild member.","gear":"Crafted gear with Civa and Fat Dwarfs.","story":"Day Four — By the Light, I believe my sword has seen less work today than my fishing rod!\n\nNot that I have been idle. Far from it. \nThere are many ways in which a knight may improve himself, and I have come to believe that a good meal and a well-stocked pack are nearly as important as a sharpened blade.\n\nMuch of my time was spent attending to the quieter arts of adventuring. I devoted myself to both fishing and cooking, bringing each profession to 150 out of 150.\n\nA modest achievement, perhaps, compared with the slaying of great beasts or the taking of enemy strongholds. \nYet I take considerable pride in it. A man who can catch his own supper and prepare it properly need not depend entirely upon the generosity of an innkeeper.\n\nThe shadowed forests of Duskwood continued to demand much of my attention.\n\nI spent a considerable part of the day carrying out the duties that remained there, sometimes in the company of Lilbigmenace Thornwhisper, and at other times alongside Furrassic Maul.\n\nThough the two did not accompany me together, I was grateful for each of their company.\n\nDuskwood is no cheerful place. Its twisted trees and perpetual gloom seem determined to remind every traveller that danger may lurk just beyond the lantern's light. Nevertheless, we pressed on, completing a great deal of the work that had brought me into those troubled woods.\n\nMy travels also carried me once more to the great dwarven city of Ironforge.\n\nThere, I had arranged to lend a hand to a fellow guild member by the name of Bu Shwacker, who had encountered some difficulties with equipment and crafting.\n\nHaving devoted no small amount of time to such matters myself, I was pleased to offer what assistance I could.\n\nA Knight's strength is not measured solely by the enemies he defeats. \nThere is honour, too, in helping a companion prepare for the battles that lie ahead.\n\nAnd I have found that the bonds forged between adventurers may prove every bit as enduring as the steel upon an anvil.\n\nMy business with the guild did not end there.\n\nIn Stormwind, I encountered another of our company, a fellow known as Fat Dwarfs.\nIt was there that my shield received a rather formidable improvement: he fitted it with spikes.\n\nA most excellent addition!\n\nMy travels also brought me to the Wetlands, where I met Civa Warrior outside Menethil Harbor.\nOur meeting was concerned in part with the making of equipment, for the forge and the craftsman's trade remain important companions to the sword.\n\nYet, as often happens when warriors find themselves in one another's company, peaceful industry eventually gave way to a test of arms.\n\nCiva and I crossed weapons in a friendly duel outside the harbor.\nThe contest ended in my favour. \n\nThere was also a journey north into Silverpine Forest, where my business lay within the walls of Shadowfang Keep.\n\nMy visit was brief, and I did not linger long among those dark woods.\n\nSome roads deserve an entire chapter. \nOthers are best remembered simply as places where duty carried one's feet before the next adventure called.\n\nAs the day wore on, I resolved to travel south toward Booty Bay.\nNow, a sensible traveller might have sought a proper road, a ship, or some other respectable means of passage.\n\nI, however, chose to swim.\n\nSetting out from the coast of Westfall, I made my way southward through the waters, following the shoreline toward the distant port.\n\nIt was a long journey, and not one I would recommend to a man particularly fond of dry boots.\nMore surprising still were the many members of the Horde whom I encountered along the way.\n\nOne might reasonably expect such meetings to end with drawn weapons and considerable unpleasantness. \nYet the road or, in this case, the sea has a peculiar way of bringing together those who would ordinarily stand upon opposite sides of a battlefield.\n\nAt one camp, I found myself among a remarkable gathering of Horde adventurers.\n\nWhat a strange sight we must have made! \nA knight of the Alliance, far from home, standing in the company of those whom the banners of our factions would name enemies.\n\nSometimes the strangest adventures are the ones in which nobody draws a sword.\n\nAt long last, my journey brought me to Booty Bay.\nAfter so many miles of swimming, travelling, questing, and tending to my crafts, the sight of the harbor was a welcome one indeed.\n\nI made certain to secure the local flight path, knowing full well that I had little desire to repeat that entire journey through the water merely to return.\n\nAnd there, in Booty Bay, my fourth day upon the road came to its end.\n\nI had advanced my trades, served alongside trusted companions, helped fellow guild members with their equipment, crossed blades with another warrior, ventured into Shadowfang Keep, and travelled farther south than I had yet imagined.\n\nNo great crown was won. No kingdom was restored.\nBut the world had grown larger, my friendships stronger, and the Chronicle of House Mardenholt another page longer.\n\nThe Oath Outlives the Crown.\n\n— Ser Atte Mardenholt","outcome":"New gear crafted.\nQuesting nearly done in Duskwood. \nWon a Duel. \nHelped a guild member.","photos":[{"src":"assets/chronicle-004-001.webp","caption":"Horde camp in westfall"},{"src":"assets/chronicle-004-002.webp","caption":"Duel with Civa "},{"src":"assets/chronicle-004-003.webp","caption":"Crafting with Bu Shwacker"}]},{"title":"Chapter V — Of Dark Waters and Ancient Bones","date":"2026-11-08","zone":"Darkshore · The Master's Glaive · Ashenvale · Blackfathom Deeps · Duskwood · Wetlands · Stormwind City","companions":"Quillb Picklepocket, Civa Warrior, Spagetoll Cuh","quests":"Discovered the Master's Glaive in Darkshore.\nWalked to Blackfathom Deeps in Ashenvale and completed the dungeon with little difficulty.\nCompleted Duskwood's remaining quests.\nCompleted quests with guild members in the Wetlands to prepare for the Stormwind Stockades the next day.\nFinished the evening in an inn in Stormwind.","gear":"No notable equipment or crafting achievements recorded.","story":"Day Five — I am beginning to suspect that Azeroth has more forgotten horrors buried beneath its soil than any sensible kingdom ought to possess.\n\nAnd today, I believe I found one of the largest.\n\nTHE MASTER'S GLAIVE — A RELIC OF FORGOTTEN AGES\n\nMy journey began upon the gloomy shores of Darkshore, a land of ancient trees, restless winds, and a silence that seemed to conceal a thousand forgotten tales.\n\nIt was there that I stumbled upon a place known as the Master's Glaive.\n\nI have witnessed many peculiar sights since leaving the familiar lands of my youth, but nothing quite prepared me for what lay before me.\n\nBeneath the towering trees rested the enormous remains of some monstrous creature, its skull greater than any beast I could imagine.\n\nAnd driven into those ancient bones was a weapon of equally impossible proportions.\n\nA great blade, surrounded by an eerie blue radiance, stood as though its wielder had struck one final, terrible blow and simply left it there.\n\nI stood for some time in silence. What manner of creature had once carried such a monstrous head? And more troubling still, what manner of warrior possessed the strength to slay it?\n\nMy thoughts wandered to the old tales of the Titans and their mighty servants, those ancient beings said to have shaped the world long before the kingdoms of men arose.\n\nCould this place be the remains of some great battle fought in an age long forgotten? I knew not.\n\nYet I could not shake the feeling that I stood before something far older, and perhaps far more terrible, than anything I had encountered upon my travels.\n\nIt was a humbling sight. I have always believed a sharp sword and a stout shield capable of solving a great many problems.\n\nBut standing before that enormous skull, I began to suspect there were certain problems for which my sword would be rather insufficient.\n\nA most unsettling thought.\n\nStill, I had roads yet to travel, and so I left the ancient remains to their silent vigil beneath the trees.\n\nINTO THE BLACKFATHOM DEEPS\n\nFrom Darkshore, my travels continued on foot toward Ashenvale, where another challenge awaited beneath the ancient forest: a place known as Blackfathom Deeps.\n\nWith a name like that, one might reasonably expect terrible dangers, unspeakable creatures, and a considerable likelihood of meeting an unpleasant end.\n\nNaturally, I went inside.\n\nThe passages were dark, the surroundings grim, and the place possessed every quality one might desire in a thoroughly disagreeable dungeon.\n\nYet to my considerable surprise, the expedition proved remarkably straightforward.\n\nWe fought our way through and completed the dungeon with little difficulty.\n\nI had prepared myself for a struggle worthy of the most dramatic tales sung by wandering bards. Instead, victory came rather easily.\n\nI suppose I ought not to sound disappointed. A knight should be grateful when danger proves less dangerous than advertised.\n\nAnd I certainly shall not complain about emerging victorious with my dignity intact.\n\nPerhaps the Light smiled upon us.\n\nOr perhaps we were simply rather good at our work.\n\nI prefer to believe the latter.\n\nFAREWELL TO DUSKWOOD\n\nWith the dark depths of Ashenvale behind me, I returned to the Eastern Kingdoms and the familiar gloom of Duskwood.\n\nAh, Duskwood. A forest where sunlight appears to be considered an unwelcome visitor.\n\nI had spent a considerable portion of my recent adventures beneath its twisted branches, attending to the many troubles of its inhabitants. Its roads had become familiar, though never particularly inviting.\n\nBut this day was different.\n\nAt long last, I finished the remaining tasks that had kept me wandering through those shadowed woods.\n\nMy work in Duskwood was complete.\n\nI confess to feeling a considerable measure of satisfaction.\n\nIt is a fine thing to look upon a land that once seemed full of unfinished business and know that one may finally depart with one's duties fulfilled.\n\nThe forest, I suspect, will remain every bit as gloomy without me. But I shall sleep better knowing my work there is done.\n\nAnd perhaps somewhere beyond those dark trees there exists a country where the sun shines for more than a few moments at a time.\n\nA man may dream.\n\nFELLOWSHIP IN THE WETLANDS\n\nMy travels next brought me to the Wetlands, where I was reunited with familiar members of our guild.\n\nQuilby Picklepocket, Civa Warrior, and Spagetoll Cuh were once more at my side.\n\nA welcome sight indeed. There is something reassuring about seeing familiar faces after days spent wandering through ancient ruins and haunted forests.\n\nTogether, we set about completing the quests required for our next undertaking.\n\nFor our attention had turned toward a new destination: the Stockades of Stormwind.\n\nIt seems that even beneath the great walls of our own capital, trouble has found a place to make itself comfortable.\n\nA most inconsiderate arrangement. One would think the authorities might have selected a more peaceful location for their prisoners.\n\nNevertheless, our preparations required further work, and so we spent our time among the marshes of the Wetlands, attending to the tasks before us.\n\nThe ground was damp, the surroundings disagreeable, and I suspect my boots shall never fully recover.\n\nYet such inconveniences are easily endured in good company.\n\nWhat began as chance meetings upon the roads of Azeroth has gradually become a fellowship of familiar companions. And I have come to appreciate that more than I might once have expected.\n\nWith our preparations completed, we resolved to face the Stockades on the following day.\n\nA NIGHT BENEATH STORMWIND'S WALLS\n\nAt last, with another long day behind me, I made my way back to Stormwind.\n\nThe sight of its great stone walls was most welcome.\n\nAfter ancient forests, monstrous skulls, dark dungeons, haunted roads, and the endless damp of the Wetlands, the simple comforts of civilisation seemed a blessing beyond measure.\n\nI sought out an inn and found myself at last beneath a proper roof.\n\nTomorrow would bring another descent into darkness, this time beneath the very city whose walls offered me shelter.\n\nA strange thought, that danger should wait so close to the comforts of home.\n\nYet that is a concern for the morrow.\n\nTonight, I intend to enjoy a warm meal, a comfortable bed, and perhaps a drink to celebrate the day's accomplishments.\n\nFor today I witnessed the remains of an ancient horror, conquered the depths of Blackfathom, finished my duties in Duskwood, and stood once more beside good companions.\n\nNot a poor day's work, if I may say so myself.\n\nAnd tomorrow? Tomorrow, we shall see what manner of trouble awaits within the Stockades.\n\nBut for now, I believe this knight has earned his rest.\n\nSer Atte Mardenholt\n\nThe Oath Outlives the Crown.\n\n—\n\nARCHIVIST'S NOTE — THE FATE OF SOGGOTH\n\nThe following account belongs to later Warcraft historical lore and represents knowledge beyond what Ser Atte personally discovered.\n\nThe colossal remains at the Master's Glaive belong to Soggoth the Slitherer, an ancient herald of the Old Gods.\n\nAccording to ancient tablets uncovered by archaeologists, the Titans dispatched twenty stone giants to confront the monstrous creature. Nineteen fell during the struggle.\n\nThe final survivor, Kronn, allowed Soggoth's enormous tentacles to seize him and draw him close enough to strike.\n\nWith his mighty sword, Kronn delivered the fatal blow, leaving the weapon embedded in Soggoth's skull.\n\nYet the creature's destruction did not entirely end its influence. The Twilight's Hammer cult later sought the dark power believed to linger within its remains.\n\nThus the Master's Glaive stands as both a monument to an ancient victory and a reminder that some evils may endure long after their defeat.","outcome":"Blackfathom Deeps completed. Duskwood completed. Master's Glaive discovered. Stockades planned for the following day; the night ended at Stormwind's inn.","photos":[{"src":"assets/editor-2026-11-21/chapter-05-plate-01.webp","caption":"the Master's Glaive"}]},{"title":"Chapter VI — Of Broken Chains and Distant Shores","date":"2026-11-09","zone":"Darkmoon Faire · Stormwind City · The Stockade · Silverpine Forest · Shadowfang Keep · Wetlands · Excavation Site: Wetlands · Ratchet · The Barrens · Wailing Caverns · Fray Island · Westfall · Stonetalon Mountains · Dun Morogh · Gnomeregan · Ironforge","companions":"Furrassic Maul, Melan Holya, Thundrus Buccaklapper","quests":"Earned Darkmoon Faire tickets; defeated every named boss in the Stockade, Shadowfang Keep, Excavation Site: Wetlands, Wailing Caverns and Gnomeregan; completed The Affray with Thundrus on Fray Island and learned Berserker Stance and Intercept; pursued mysterious Sleeping Bag clues through Westfall, the Barrens and Stonetalon Mountains without finishing the quest.","gear":"Cloak of Hermitic Bliss from Kresh in Wailing Caverns: +33% swimming speed, −25% movement speed on land; reserved exclusively for swimming. Learned Berserker Stance and Intercept.","story":"There are days upon the road when a man may find an hour to rest, tend his armour, and reflect upon the trials he has endured.\n\nAnd then there are days when the road seems determined to see how much one knight can accomplish before nightfall.\n\nThis was very much the latter.\n\nBefore I found rest beneath the mountains of Khaz Modan, I would venture through five dungeons, cross the Great Sea, endure a trial of arms, follow mysterious messages across two continents, and walk alone through a snowstorm. Somewhere along the way I would also find a cloak that made me extraordinarily pleased with the thought of swimming.\n\nA MORNING AMONG THE DARKMOON BANNERS\n\nI began my travels not with the clash of steel, but beneath the lively banners of the Darkmoon Faire outside Stormwind. Strange tents and cheerful music filled the woodland clearing; merchants and performers competed for the attention of the curious, and I was pleased for once to visit somewhere that did not immediately attempt to kill me.\n\nI explored the grounds and earned a handful of Darkmoon Faire tickets. I could not yet say what they might purchase, but a knight should occasionally permit himself a harmless amusement.\n\nThe day's peace was not destined to last.\n\nTHE PRISON BENEATH STORMWIND\n\nMy first expedition brought me together with Furrassic Maul, the night elven druid whose company had become familiar, and a new acquaintance: Melan Holya, a human priestess of the Plot Armor Crew. It seemed a suitably memorable first meeting to venture together into the Stockade, where Stormwind's prisoners had seized their own gaol.\n\nThe rebellion was linked to the Defias Brotherhood, whose shadows I had already encountered in Westfall. We defeated Targorr the Dread, a brutal orc, and Kam Deepfury, a dangerous dwarven inmate. Hamhock, a towering ogre, proved that the narrow passages of a prison offer remarkably little room in which to escape the blows of a great brute. Dextren Ward soon joined the defeated.\n\nAt last we confronted Bazil Thredd, whose dealings with the Defias made his imprisonment a matter of particular concern to the crown. Together we overcame him, completing our conquest of all five principal foes.\n\nI emerged into the light reflecting on the curious truth that a kingdom may build mighty walls against enemies from without while rebellion gathers beneath its streets.\n\nTHE WOLVES OF SHADOWFANG\n\nOur next destination lay in Silverpine Forest, where Shadowfang Keep rose above the troubled village of Pyrewood. Once a seat of Lordaeron's nobility, it had become the refuge of Archmage Arugal. In desperation to combat the undead, he had called forth the worgen; his supposed salvation had become another curse.\n\nFurrassic, Melan, and I entered the dark fortress. We brought down Rethilgore the jailer and Razorclaw the Butcher. The restless spirit of Baron Silverlaine reminded me how many noble halls of Lordaeron now sheltered only ghosts. Commander Springvale and Odo the Blindwatcher likewise fell before us.\n\nFarther within, Fenrus the Devourer and Wolf Master Nandos guarded the approach to their master's chambers. Neither could hold us back.\n\nFinally we confronted Archmage Arugal himself, the learned man who had come to embrace the monsters born of his own misguided work. His spells were formidable, but our united efforts prevailed. All eight of the keep's great foes were defeated.\n\nI left those haunted walls troubled by the thought that good intentions, when wedded to pride and desperation, may give rise to evils as terrible as deliberate cruelty.\n\nTHE SECRETS BENEATH THE WETLANDS\n\nBefore crossing the sea, we ventured into the Wetlands, where an excavation had uncovered ancient workings associated with the Titans. The Explorer's League had cause for wonder, though the marsh surrounding their discoveries offered more immediate reasons for caution.\n\nWe fought Saltspine, an enormous crocolisk, and Shadetooth, a vicious raptor. The Highland Horror showed that even the vegetation might turn against an incautious traveler.\n\nDeeper among the unearthed structures waited the Relic Guardian, an ancient construct preserving secrets from a forgotten age. Its strength could not prevent us from overcoming it. Four great opponents lay defeated, and the excavation was ours to leave behind.\n\nI suspected the dwarves would be back with more picks, more questions, and a determination to dig somewhere even more dangerous.\n\nTHE WAILING DEPTHS\n\nAcross the Great Sea we traveled to Ratchet in the Barrens, and from that port set forth to explore the Wailing Caverns. Beneath the dry plains, the druid Naralex had sought to restore life through the Emerald Dream. Yet the Emerald Nightmare had corrupted his work, twisting his followers into the Druids of the Fang.\n\nWe fought Lady Anacondra, Lord Cobrahn, Lord Pythas, and Lord Serpentis, all fallen guardians of the disturbed caverns. Skum the thunder lizard and Verdan the Everliving, whose unnatural growth bore witness to the spreading corruption, also fell before us.\n\nAmong the deeper chambers, however, we met Kresh, a great turtle whose defeat yielded a treasure unlike any I had ever hoped to possess: the Cloak of Hermitic Bliss.\n\nIt increased my swimming speed by thirty-three percent.\n\nThirty-three!\n\nAdmittedly, it also reduced my speed on dry land by twenty-five percent. This was not a garment suited to ordinary travel, and I had no intention of becoming the slowest-moving knight in the Eastern Kingdoms merely for the pleasure of wearing it.\n\nI resolved to use it for swimming, and only for swimming. Should a river, lake, or sufficiently deep puddle lie before me, I would enter it with the confidence of a man possessed of an entirely unreasonable advantage over other travelers.\n\nOur work was not yet finished. At Dreamer's Rock, in the shadow of Naralex's troubled slumber, we faced Mutanus the Devourer, the monstrous final manifestation of the caverns' corruption. Together we brought him down, completing our defeat of all eight great enemies within the Wailing Caverns.\n\nThe caverns were won. I had a remarkable cloak. I shall confess that I could not easily decide which triumph pleased me more.\n\nTHE AFFRAY — A WARRIOR'S TRIAL\n\nAfter so many journeys beneath the earth, I took a temporary leave of dungeon expeditions and joined Thundrus Buccaklapper—Cheek, as I commonly call him—for the warrior's trial on Fray Island off the Barrens.\n\nUnder the watch of Klannoc Macleod, we entered the contest called the Affray. Challenger after challenger came against us. Cheek fought at my side, his two swords flashing, while I met the opposition with my own blade and shield.\n\nAt last Big Will stood before us, a formidable opponent whose size and strength might have disheartened less determined warriors. We fought him together, and our victory came without great difficulty.\n\nI earned Berserker Stance and the ability to Intercept my enemies. I had been taught from childhood that anger must serve a warrior's duty, never rule it. On Fray Island I learned something of how to channel that fury when the moment demanded.\n\nTHE TRAIL OF THE BURNED NOTES\n\nCheek and I next followed a curious trail of messages that began in Westfall. An enigmatic clue directed us across the world toward a burned tower in the Barrens, where another fragment awaited us among the ruins. From there the trail led onward into the Stonetalon Mountains and a further note at an abandoned site.\n\nWho could have placed these messages at such distant locations, and for what purpose?\n\nThe unknown author evidently possessed both a love of mystery and a profound disregard for the inconvenience of travel.\n\nWe had not yet reached the end of the trail. For now I kept its unanswered questions with me, resolved to seek the next clue upon another day.\n\nTHROUGH THE SILENCE OF DUN MOROGH\n\nLater, on the road toward Gnomeregan, I walked alone through Dun Morogh. Snow began to fall and soon thickened into a storm that swallowed the distant pines. The path faded under fresh snow; the forest became shadows and the world narrowed to the ground beneath my boots.\n\nI raised a torch against the gloom. Its small flame was poor company beside so vast a silence, yet it was enough.\n\nI thought of Greybough Hall and the fires once kept burning for travelers in bitter weather. I remembered my father and the charge entrusted to the wardens of our house. A man need not stand against an army to require protection. Sometimes the winter road itself is danger enough.\n\nWith the torch before me, I continued, and in time the path emerged from the whiteness once more.\n\nGNOMEREGAN — THE CITY THAT FELL\n\nBeyond the storm, I reunited with Furrassic and Melan for our final undertaking: Gnomeregan, the great underground city of the gnomes, lost to troggs, poison, and betrayal.\n\nIn its ruined galleries we defeated Grubbis, a fearsome trogg, and Viscous Fallout, a terrible consequence of the contamination that had consumed the city. Electrocutioner 6000 and Crowd Pummeler 9-60 showed that the machines once built in wonder had become instruments of war.\n\nWe overcame the Mechanical Menagerie, and at last reached Mekgineer Thermaplugg, whose ambition and treachery had helped bring Gnomeregan to ruin. His engines could not save him. Together we defeated the traitor, completing our conquest of every great encounter within the city.\n\nIn those poisoned halls I thought again of Lordaeron. Different peoples, different histories, yet the sorrow of a lost homeland was familiar.\n\nTHE FIRES OF IRONFORGE\n\nAt last I reached Ironforge, grateful for its enduring stone and the warmth of its great forges.\n\nFive dungeons lay behind me: the Stockade, Shadowfang Keep, Excavation Site: Wetlands, Wailing Caverns, and Gnomeregan. With Furrassic and Melan I had faced every great foe within them. I had welcomed a new companion, shared a warrior's trial with Cheek, earned Berserker Stance, and followed a strange series of notes across the world.\n\nI had walked alone through a mountain storm, with only a torch and the memories of my lost home.\n\nAnd beneath the Barrens I had discovered a cloak that made me thirty-three percent faster in water. I considered this last achievement particularly splendid.\n\nYet as the night settled over Ironforge, the unfinished trail of messages returned to my thoughts. Somewhere beyond the mountains, another clue was waiting, and with it perhaps the answer to our mystery.\n\nFor now, I would rest. The road could trouble me again tomorrow.\n\nThe Oath Outlives the Crown.","outcome":"Rested in Ironforge after clearing five dungeons. Melan Holya joined the company; the mystery of the scattered notes remains unfinished.","photos":[{"src":"assets/editor-2026-11-21/chapter-06-plate-01.webp","caption":"Darkmoon Fair"},{"src":"assets/editor-2026-11-21/chapter-06-plate-02.webp","caption":"Snowstorm"},{"src":"assets/editor-2026-11-21/chapter-06-plate-03.webp","caption":"Warrior Quest with Thundrus. "}]},{"title":"Chapter VII — Of Forged Steel and Violet Shadows","date":"2026-11-10","zone":"Ironforge · Stormwind City · Hillsbrad Foothills · Southshore · Darkshore · Stonetalon Mountains · Nijel's Point · Desolace · City of Dalaran","companions":"Fat Dwarfs, Thundrus Buccaklapper, Melan Holya, Spagetoll Cuh, Old Warden","quests":"Celebrated Fat Dwarfs' new bear mount in Stormwind; blacksmithing; won a friendly duel against Thundrus outside Southshore; sailed to Darkshore, flew to Stonetalon Peak and reached Nijel's Point; quested against naga and centaurs in Desolace, fished at sunset and spotted a wanderer believed to be Rexxar with Misha; completed a City of Dalaran dungeon expedition with four guildmates, first meeting Old Warden.","gear":"Fat Dwarfs fitted metal spikes to Atte's shield. Practiced blacksmithing; other ordinary equipment improvements were not singled out.","story":"There are mornings when a man rises with a clear purpose, and others when he is content simply to hear the fires burning and know that, for another day, he has survived the road.\n\nI woke in Ironforge.\n\nAfter the remarkable exertions of the previous day, the dwarven city offered all that I could ask of a refuge: stout stone, warm hearths, and the comforting sound of craftsmen already busy at their anvils. For a little while I could almost persuade myself that no further adventure would be required.\n\nAlmost.\n\nA DWARF, A MOUNT, AND A SHARPER SHIELD\n\nI made my way to Stormwind, where I had arranged to meet Fat Dwarfs of the Plot Armor Crew.\n\nMy companion had only lately risen from his own sleep, but he had reason enough to wake in excellent spirits: at last, he had acquired his mount.\n\nI found him upon a great bear in the busy streets of Stormwind. The animal was an impressive beast, broad of shoulder and rather better equipped for travel than any knight walking on his own two feet. I greeted Fat and congratulated him on this happy achievement. The acquisition of a mount may seem a modest victory beside the defeat of monsters, yet anyone who has worn through a pair of boots upon the roads of Azeroth knows its true worth.\n\nI was genuinely pleased for him.\n\nThere was more to our meeting than celebration. Fat had brought his craftsman's attention to my shield, and he fitted it with metal spikes, making an already serviceable piece of equipment considerably more intimidating.\n\nMy father taught me that a shield exists to protect the man behind it. I should think he would approve of one that also gives an attacker a reason to reconsider his decisions.\n\nWe stood together for a while amid the bustle of Stormwind, an Alliance knight and his dwarven companion, admiring the fruits of a good morning's work.\n\nTHE HONEST WORK OF THE FORGE\n\nAfterward I devoted some time to blacksmithing.\n\nI have discovered that the forge can be a place of extraordinary peace. This may sound strange to anyone who has heard the deafening ring of hammer upon iron, yet there is a rhythm to the work that leaves little room for troublesome thoughts.\n\nSteel does not care who my father was, nor what became of my home. It cannot be impressed by a title, and it is entirely indifferent to the tragedies of Lordaeron.\n\nIt asks only that the smith apply heat, patience, and a reasonably steady hand.\n\nFor a time, I was glad to exchange the uncertainty of the road for something that might be shaped through honest effort. I worked at the anvil without grand designs, pleased simply to practice a craft whose results can be held in one's hand.\n\nAnd when I had finished, I found I was ready for the road once more.\n\nSWORDS DRAWN IN FRIENDSHIP\n\nMy travels brought me north into Hillsbrad Foothills, to the green country outside Southshore. There I met Thundrus Buccaklapper—Cheek, as I commonly call him.\n\nOnly the day before we had fought shoulder to shoulder against Big Will upon Fray Island, earning the discipline of the Berserker. Today, however, we agreed to discover what would happen if our blades were turned toward one another.\n\nIt was to be a friendly duel, nothing more.\n\nThere was no dispute of honor between us, no quarrel over spoils, and no lord whose judgment we sought. We were simply two warriors curious to test their skills in fair contest.\n\nCheek came at me with his two swords, fighting as eagerly as ever. I met him with sword and shield, guarding against one blade while watching the other. The field rang with the sound of steel, and more than once I had reason to thank the maker of my armor.\n\nThe contest was spirited. Neither of us surrendered the advantage easily.\n\nYet the moment came when I found an opening, pressed forward, and claimed victory.\n\nThe duel was mine.\n\nI confess to a certain satisfaction in writing those words.\n\nCheek took his defeat in good humor, and our friendship suffered no injury. In fact, the sight of him recovering upon the grass gave me cause to smile—and, I suspect, gave him every intention of demanding a rematch.\n\nA victory over a friend is pleasing. Still more pleasing is the knowledge that he remains a friend when it is over.\n\nWESTWARD ACROSS THE GREAT SEA\n\nWith the contest concluded, I turned once more toward Kalimdor.\n\nFrom Southshore I boarded a vessel bound for Darkshore. The passage carried me away from the familiar coast of the Eastern Kingdoms toward the forests and highlands of a continent still strange to me.\n\nFrom Darkshore, I travelled by air to Stonetalon Peak. The mountains rose beneath me, their wooded heights giving way to rugged paths and distant valleys. I descended from the peak and made my way toward the Alliance outpost of Nijel's Point.\n\nBeyond it lay Desolace.\n\nThe very name seemed to describe the country before I set foot upon its barren plains.\n\nI had come to quest among its scattered inhabitants, explore its shores, and indulge a pastime that had grown dear to me: fishing.\n\nTHE DESOLATE COUNTRY\n\nDesolace is a hard land, marked by ancient bones, pale stone, windswept hills and the enduring hostility of its inhabitants.\n\nCentaur clans range across its wastes, divided by feuds that appear older than any traveler can remember. Their rivalries have left little peace in a country already possessed of precious little kindness.\n\nAlong the coastline lurk the naga, serpentine descendants of the ancient night elven empire. Their history is one of pride, catastrophe, and an empire swallowed beneath the sea. Whatever grief their ancestors endured, the creatures themselves showed no particular eagerness to grant me passage.\n\nI went about my tasks among the naga and centaurs, sword at hand when needed and eyes upon the paths ahead.\n\nAt times I wondered how so much conflict could endure in a place that offered so little worth fighting over.\n\nPerhaps that is precisely how old grudges are preserved. Once a people forget why they began their struggle, they may find themselves unable to imagine how to end it.\n\nTHE WANDERER AND HIS BEAR\n\nIt was while travelling through Desolace that I beheld a sight I shall not soon forget.\n\nAn orcish figure wandered the road with a great bear at his side.\n\nThe stranger moved with the unhurried confidence of one who had long ago ceased to fear the wilderness. The bear followed not like a chained servant but like a trusted companion. There was loyalty between them, and the kind of understanding that requires few words.\n\nI did not approach them.\n\nI am a knight of the Alliance, and the banners of the Horde are not those under which I have sworn my service. Yet the sight brought to mind stories I had heard of Rexxar, the wandering Mok'Nathal champion of the Horde, and Misha, his faithful bear.\n\nPerhaps it was he.\n\nIf so, the road had placed me within sight of a warrior whose name is spoken with reverence far beyond the lands he patrols. Whatever our differences, I could respect a man who seems to have found a truer fellowship among the beasts of the wild than many noblemen find in their courts.\n\nThe wanderer continued upon his way.\n\nI continued upon mine.\n\nAnd between us lay the strange, uneasy peace that sometimes exists when two enemies have no desire to become enemies that day.\n\nFISHING AT THE END OF THE SUN\n\nAs the hours passed, I found my way to the water.\n\nThere, beneath the great sky of Desolace, I laid aside the concerns of battle and turned to fishing.\n\nI have come to value these quiet intervals more than I once thought possible.\n\nA fish does not inquire whether one has been knighted. It does not care which house one serves, which road one has travelled, or how many enemies one has overcome.\n\nIt merely decides, with extraordinary stubbornness, whether one's bait deserves attention.\n\nI find this refreshing.\n\nThe evening sun sank toward the horizon. Amber light spread across the sea, and the ruins standing far out among the waters became silhouettes against a sky of rose and gold.\n\nFor a little while, I stood without moving.\n\nI thought of Greybough Hall, of the old oak, and of evenings when the road had seemed a distant thing. I wondered whether my family had ever watched a sky so splendid, and whether I should have noticed such ordinary beauty more carefully when I had the chance.\n\nDesolace is not a gentle country.\n\nYet it offered me a moment of unexpected peace, and I was grateful for it.\n\nTHE FELLOWSHIP GATHERS\n\nLater, the quiet gave way once more to the business of adventuring.\n\nI joined four members of the Plot Armor Crew for a dungeon expedition: Melan Holya, whose skill as a priestess I had come to trust; Thundrus Buccaklapper, my recent opponent and frequent companion; and Spagetoll Cuh, the human warlock whose path had crossed mine upon my very first day.\n\nThe fourth was someone new.\n\nHer name was Old Warden, a dwarven priestess and another member of our guild. This was our first meeting.\n\nI knew little of her history, but I was glad to make her acquaintance. There is something reassuring about a fellowship that continues to find new companions, especially when one's next destination is a place known for perilous magic.\n\nFor we had gathered to enter the City of Dalaran.\n\nTHE VIOLET SHADOWS OF DALARAN\n\nFew cities carry a greater weight of memory than Dalaran.\n\nThe Kirin Tor once made it a renowned seat of learning, a place where mages studied the mysteries of the arcane and preserved knowledge gathered from the farthest reaches of the world. Yet even their power could not spare the city the devastation of the Third War.\n\nIts history touched the fate of Lordaeron, and so I could not look upon its violet halls without remembering what had been lost in the north.\n\nOur company ventured within the City of Dalaran dungeon, among carved chambers, glowing runes and the strange light of restless magic.\n\nMelan and Old Warden brought the steadiness of the priesthood. Spagetoll possessed arts I have never pretended to understand. Cheek and I, being warriors, relied upon the somewhat more familiar virtues of steel, strength and the ability to stand firmly between danger and one's companions.\n\nWhatever lay within those chambers, we faced the expedition together.\n\nI shall not attempt to name every foe we encountered; the day was long, and my memory of the dungeon's individual guardians is less certain than my memory of the fellowship beside me.\n\nBut I remember the atmosphere of that place: the weight of old stone, the arcane glow upon our armor, and the strange feeling of walking through a city whose greatest triumphs and greatest sorrows were written upon the same walls.\n\nI thought of Lordaeron once again.\n\nSo many homes had fallen, and so many people struggled to restore what remained.\n\nPerhaps that is why the road continues to call to me. I cannot rebuild every ruined hall or save every threatened city.\n\nBut I can carry my oath into the next place that needs a sword.\n\nTHE CITY AT DAY'S END\n\nIf memory serves, I ended the evening in Stormwind.\n\nThe day had carried me from Ironforge's forges to the streets of the human capital, from a friendly duel in Hillsbrad to the heights of Stonetalon and the bleak shores of Desolace, and at last into the arcane passages of Dalaran.\n\nFat Dwarfs had gained his mount and strengthened my shield.\n\nCheek and I had crossed blades in friendship, and, by the Light, I had won.\n\nI had worked at the forge, fished beneath a magnificent sunset, and glimpsed a great wanderer with a bear upon the lonely roads of Kalimdor.\n\nI had met Old Warden and ventured beside her and our guildmates into the Violet City's mysteries.\n\nIt had been a full day, though not every memorable hour had been spent in battle. Some of its finest moments had belonged to friendship, patient craft, and the silence of the evening sea.\n\nThe mysterious trail of notes from our previous journey still awaited an answer. There would be other roads and other trials, and I had little doubt that tomorrow would find a fresh means of troubling me.\n\nFor tonight, however, I would rest.\n\nThe Oath Outlives the Crown.","outcome":"After the Desolace journey and City of Dalaran dungeon, Atte believes he rested in Stormwind. The mysterious trail of notes remains unfinished.","photos":[{"src":"assets/editor-2026-11-21/chapter-07-plate-01.webp","caption":"Duel with Thundrus"},{"src":"assets/editor-2026-11-21/chapter-07-plate-02.webp","caption":"Desolace "},{"src":"assets/editor-2026-11-21/chapter-07-plate-03.webp","caption":"Fat Dwarfs with he's mount in Stormwind. "},{"src":"assets/editor-2026-11-21/chapter-07-plate-04.webp","caption":"Dalaran with PAC "}]},{"title":"Chapter VIII — Of Scarlet Zeal and Thornbound Paths","date":"2026-11-11","zone":"Western Plaguelands, Tirisfal Glades, Scarlet Monastery, Razorfen Kraul, The Barrens, Stranglethorn Vale","companions":"Melan Holya, Quillb Picklepocket, Spagetoll Cuh, Thundrus Buccaklapper","quests":"Travelled through the Western Plaguelands to Scarlet Monastery. Cleared the Graveyard and Library with Melan Holya. Later joined guildmates for a successful Razorfen Kraul dungeon run, arriving by magical summons. Finished the evening fishing along the coast of Stranglethorn Vale.","gear":"Collected ordinary dungeon loot and equipment upgrades throughout the day. No particularly memorable or significant items were recorded.","story":"Day VIII - There are roads a man walks because duty demands it, and others he follows simply because adventure has become a habit he can no longer easily abandon.\n\nThis morning, I found myself returning toward a land whose name has never ceased to stir something within me.\n\nLordaeron.\n\nIf my memory serves, I had spent the previous night in Stormwind. From there, I made my way north toward Chillwind Camp, the Alliance outpost standing at the edge of the Western Plaguelands.\n\nIt was a strange feeling to travel into those troubled lands again.\n\nThe Western Plaguelands are not merely another wilderness to me. They belong to the country of my birth, to the kingdom whose banners once flew above my family's home, and to memories that have followed me farther than any road.\n\nSomewhere beyond those scarred fields lie the lands where House Mardenholt once kept its watch.\n\nI have learned that returning to familiar country does not always bring comfort.\n\nSometimes it merely reminds a man of what is missing.\nYet I had no intention of seeking the ruins of my home that day.\n\nI had arranged to meet Melan Holya, my fellow adventurer of the Plot Armor Crew, for an expedition into one of the strangest strongholds remaining in northern Lordaeron.\n\nFrom Chillwind Camp, I continued on foot through the Western Plaguelands toward Tirisfal Glades.\nOur destination was the Scarlet Monastery.\n\nAnd I suspected that its inhabitants would be considerably less welcoming than the name suggested.\n\nThe Scarlet Monastery stands as a reminder of how even noble intentions may become something dreadful.\n\nOnce, the monastery had served the priesthood of Lordaeron. Its halls were places of study and devotion, and its walls had sheltered those who sought wisdom in the teachings of the Light.\n\nBut the coming of the Scourge changed everything.\n\nFear took root where faith had once flourished.\n\nThose who gathered beneath the Scarlet Crusade's banner swore to destroy the undead and reclaim their homeland. It was a cause that, in its beginning, I might well have understood.\n\nHad I not lost my own home to the horrors that consumed Lordaeron?\n\nHad I not sworn to defend the innocent against the darkness?\n\nYet somewhere along the way, the Scarlet Crusade had allowed its hatred to consume its judgment.\n\nTo its fanatical followers, nearly any outsider might be an enemy.\n\nThe living were scarcely safer from suspicion than the dead.\n\nA troubling thought came upon me as I approached its gates.\n\nA man may carry the symbol of righteousness upon his shield and yet forget what righteousness requires of him.\n\nI thought of my father's teachings.\n\nHe had always insisted that a knight's duty was not merely to destroy evil, but to recognize those who still deserved protection.\n\nThe distinction seemed especially important here.\nMelan and I prepared ourselves for the dangers ahead.\n\nThen we entered the monastery.\n\nOur first undertaking carried us into the Graveyard.\n\nIt was an unsettling place, even by the standards of a kingdom already haunted by the dead.\n\nThe monastery's burial grounds should have been a place of solemn remembrance. Instead, its chambers had become a refuge for cruelty, restless spirits, and the darker consequences of the Scarlet Crusade's fanaticism.\n\nThere was little peace to be found among those graves.\n\nIn the chambers beyond, Interrogator Vishas had earned a dreadful reputation for extracting confessions from those unfortunate enough to fall into his hands.\n\nI have never held much respect for men who mistake cruelty for strength.\n\nA prisoner who has been broken upon a torture rack may confess to anything. Such words reveal far more about the interrogator than about the truth.\n\nDeeper within the Graveyard lay the domain of Bloodmage Thalnos, a figure associated with the very kind of dark magic the Scarlet Crusade claimed to despise.\n\nThere was a bitter irony in that.\n\nA place devoted to destroying the undead had become haunted by horrors of its own making.\n\nMelan and I pressed through the Graveyard together.\nDespite the grim surroundings, our progress was remarkably steady.\n\nWe met the opposition before us without great difficulty and completed our undertaking far more comfortably than the monastery's reputation might have led us to expect.\n\nI was glad of Melan's company.\n\nThere is reassurance in knowing that a capable priestess stands beside a warrior in a place where both steel and faith may be tested.\n\nWhen at last we emerged from the burial grounds, I felt no particular desire to remain among the graves.\nFortunately, the monastery had another wing awaiting our attention.\n\nAnd its next guardians, I suspected, would prove rather more fond of books.\n\nOur second undertaking led us into the Scarlet Monastery's Library.\n\nOf all the places to find armed fanatics, a library might seem an unusual choice.\n\nYet the Scarlet Crusade had managed to turn even the pursuit of knowledge into an occupation requiring guards, weapons, and an alarming degree of hostility toward visitors.\n\nThere is a certain tragedy in such a thing.\n\nBooks preserve the thoughts of people long dead. They allow the wisdom of generations to survive the fall of cities and kingdoms.\n\nAfter what happened to Lordaeron, I could think of few treasures more deserving of protection.\n\nAnd yet here, learning had been placed in the service of zealotry.\n\nThe Library was defended by the crusade's soldiers and spellcasters.\n\nAmong its guardians was Houndmaster Loksey, a stern keeper of trained hounds whose loyalty to their master was surely more dependable than his hospitality toward strangers.\n\nFarther within stood Arcanist Doan, one of the monastery's powerful magicians, entrusted with protecting its deeper halls.\n\nDoan's command of the arcane made him no ordinary opponent. The Library's defenders relied not only upon swords and devotion, but upon magic powerful enough to punish careless intruders.\n\nStill, Melan and I had already found our rhythm.\n\nWe moved through the chambers with confidence, overcoming the resistance before us and bringing our second expedition to a successful conclusion.\n\nAgain, the task proved less troublesome than I had expected.\n\nI shall not pretend to have been disappointed.\n\nA knight need not suffer terribly in order to consider a day's work worthwhile.\n\nThere was also something pleasing about emerging from a place filled with armed fanatics while remaining entirely capable of walking back out under one's own power.\n\nThe Library held another curiosity: the Scarlet Key, said to be kept within a strongbox beyond Arcanist Doan's chamber. Such a key could open the monastery's Armory and Cathedral.\n\nWhether those deeper halls would become the object of a future expedition remained to be seen.\n\nFor now, Melan and I had completed the Graveyard and Library.\nTwo successful undertakings, both accomplished without any great trouble.\n\nI considered the morning well spent.\n\nLater that day, another opportunity for adventure presented itself.\n\nThis time, our destination lay far from the cold and troubled fields of Lordaeron.\n\nSeveral members of the Plot Armor Crew had gathered for an expedition into Razorfen Kraul, an underground stronghold of the quilboar in the southern Barrens.\n\nAmong the company were familiar companions.\n\nQuillb Picklepocket, our guild master and a rogue whose resourcefulness had often served the fellowship well.\n\nSpagetoll Cuh, the warlock I had known since the first days of my travels.\n\nAnd Thundrus Buccaklapper, whose twin blades had become a familiar sight beside my own sword and shield.\n\nMelan, too, had been my companion through the earlier expeditions.\n\nWhat pleased me almost as much as the promise of another dungeon was the manner in which I arrived.\n\nThe others summoned me.\n\nI cannot overstate the convenience of this arrangement.\n\nI had spent a considerable portion of my recent life crossing mountains, boarding ships, following distant roads, and discovering just how large Azeroth becomes when one is expected to travel across it on foot.\n\nAnd now, through the helpfulness of my companions, I could reach the next adventure without undertaking the journey myself.\n\nI believe this may be among the finest magical achievements known to civilized people.\n\nI am entirely in favor of heroic expeditions.\nI am even more in favor of heroic expeditions in which somebody else has already done the travelling.\n\nThus, without the customary exhaustion of a long approach, I found myself ready to join the fellowship.\n\nRazorfen Kraul awaited.\n\nThere are places in Azeroth whose history stretches so far into the past that they seem more legend than reality.\n\nRazorfen Kraul is one such place.\n\nThe quilboar who dwell within its twisted passages trace their ancestry to Agamaggan, the mighty boar demigod who fought against the Burning Legion during the War of the Ancients.\n\nIt is said that Agamaggan fell in that terrible struggle, giving his life in defense of the world.\n\nFrom the places where his blood soaked the earth, enormous thorn-covered growths arose, forming the tangled warrens known as the Razorfen.\n\nTen thousand years later, his descendants still dwell among those thorns.\n\nI found the tale strangely moving.\n\nA great creature had sacrificed himself to protect a world whose people would never truly know the cost of his courage.\n\nAnd from the ground upon which he fell, life had continued.\n\nNot peaceful life, perhaps.\n\nBut life nonetheless.\n\nI wondered what the ancient demigod might think of the tribes now fighting beneath the thorns that bear his memory.\n\nWould he recognize their struggles as worthy of his sacrifice?\nOr would he, like so many fathers, find himself rather disappointed in the behavior of his descendants?\n\nI cannot say.\n\nBut it was an unusual thought to carry into battle.\n\nOur company entered the tangled stronghold.\n\nThe passages twisted through enormous roots and thorn-covered tunnels, rising and falling through chambers inhabited by the Death's Head and Razorfen quilboar.\n\nTheir warriors and shamans had made the place their own, and the deeper warrens were guarded by creatures whose names were known to those familiar with the region.\n\nThere was Roogug, a geomancer dwelling among the earlier passages, and Aggem Thorncurse, whose command of spirits and nature made him a threat within the thorny tunnels.\n\nThe Death's Head tribe followed darker practices.\n\nAmong its leaders was Death Speaker Jargba, who wielded shadow magic and the power to turn a warrior's will against his companions.\n\nFarther within stood Overlord Ramtusk, a formidable quilboar warlord, and Agathelos the Raging, a great boar whose fury made him particularly dangerous when provoked.\n\nAt the heart of Razorfen Kraul lay the domain of Charlga Razorflank, the crone whose authority held the stronghold beneath her influence.\n\nIt was a place of strange history and stranger inhabitants.\n\nYet for all its reputation, our expedition proceeded with remarkable ease.\n\nPerhaps we had grown stronger.\n\nPerhaps our companions had become more practiced in fighting together.\n\nOr perhaps fortune had simply decided, for once, that we had earned an uncomplicated victory.\n\nWhatever the explanation, the Razorfen failed to halt us.\n\nOur company pressed through its winding passages and completed the dungeon without serious difficulty.\n\nThere were no desperate retreats to recount.\n\nNo great disasters that threatened to divide the party.\n\nOnly the steady progress of a fellowship that knew its business.\n\nAfter the uncertainty and hardship of so many earlier adventures, I found this rather agreeable.\n\nI suspect a good company should not be judged solely by the dangers it survives.\nSometimes its strength is best demonstrated by how efficiently it overcomes them.\n\nAnd ours had done precisely that.\n\nWith the day's dungeon expeditions completed, I turned toward a very different destination.\n\nStranglethorn Vale.\n\nThere are few places in Azeroth that seem more distant in spirit from the ruined fields of Lordaeron.\n\nWhere the Western Plaguelands are cold and sorrowful, Stranglethorn is warm and alive.\n\nIts jungles are filled with the calls of unfamiliar creatures, its air heavy with moisture, and its coastline washed by waters that stretch toward the distant horizon.\n\nI had travelled there before.\n\nIndeed, I had grown rather fond of the place, although I am not certain its wildlife returned the sentiment.\n\nBut this evening I had no desire to go hunting.\n\nI wanted to fish.\n\nThere is something deeply satisfying about ending a day of swordsmanship with an activity that requires almost none of it.\n\nI found a quiet place beside the water and cast my line.\n\nThe day had taken me through a monastery haunted by zealotry, into a library guarded by armed fanatics, and beneath the ancient thorns of a fallen demigod.\n\nNow there was only the sound of the sea.\n\nThe darkness gathered slowly over Stranglethorn, and the waters moved with the gentle rhythm of the tide.\n\nFor a while, I listened.\n\nI thought of the companions whose friendship had carried me through so many dangers.\n\nOf Melan, who had travelled beside me through the Scarlet Monastery.\n\nOf Quillb, Spagetoll, and Cheek, whose company had made Razorfen Kraul a far easier undertaking than the place deserved.\n\nAnd of the curious way my life had changed since setting out upon the road.\n\nThere was a time when I believed a knight's worth must be measured principally by the battles he fought.\n\nNow I was beginning to think that the quieter moments mattered just as much.\n\nA shared victory.\n\nA familiar voice.\nA peaceful evening beside the water.\n\nThese were not the great deeds that minstrels preserved in song.\n\nYet they were the things that made the journey worth remembering.\n\nAs night settled upon the southern coast, I made my way to Booty Bay.\n\nThe harbor was alive with the familiar bustle of sailors, merchants, goblins, and travelers who seemed to have brought the business of half the world into one crowded settlement.\n\nLanterns glimmered along the docks.\n\nShips rested upon the dark water.\n\nAnd somewhere among the wooden walkways, I was reasonably certain that a goblin was attempting to sell something at a price no sensible man should ever agree to pay.\n\nIt was a comforting sort of chaos.\n\nI had begun the day upon the roads of Lordaeron.\n\nI had journeyed into the Scarlet Monastery with Melan, completed two wings, joined my guildmates in the thornbound depths of Razorfen Kraul, and returned to the southern sea for a peaceful evening of fishing.\n\nThree dungeon expeditions in one day.\n\nAll completed without any great difficulty.\n\nA most agreeable result.\n\nYet as I prepared to rest, my thoughts returned to the Scarlet Monastery.\n\nI could not forget the strange contrast between the faith its people claimed to defend and the cruelty they had allowed to flourish within its walls.\n\nThe fall of Lordaeron had wounded many hearts.\n\nSome had answered that grief with compassion.\n\nOthers had turned it into hatred.\n\nI hoped that, whatever trials awaited me, I would never lose sight of the difference.\n\nFor a knight's oath means little if he forgets whom it was sworn to protect.\n\nAnd so, with the sounds of Booty Bay carrying through the night, I set aside my equipment and prepared for sleep.\n\nTomorrow, the road would call again.\n\nBut tonight, I was content.\n\nSer Atte Mardenholt\n\nThe Oath Outlives the Crown.","outcome":"Three successful dungeon expeditions with little difficulty. Strengthened friendships within the Plot Armor Crew and reflected upon the troubled legacy of Lordaeron's Scarlet Crusade. Ended the evening peacefully in Booty Bay after some late-night fishing, ready for whatever adventures the next day might bring.","photos":[{"src":"assets/chapter-08-plate-01.webp","caption":"STV Fishing. "},{"src":"assets/chapter-08-plate-02.webp","caption":"RFK"}]},{"title":"Chapter IX — Of Quiet Waters and Restless Blades","date":"2026-11-12","zone":"Stranglethorn Vale, Booty Bay, Lake Nazferiti, the Vile Reef, Stormwind City","companions":"Quillb Picklepocket, Spagetoll Cuh, Thundrus Buccaklapper, Psykopat Per","quests":"Reached Fishing and Cooking 225, improved First Aid, traded on the Auction House, completed jungle quests and fought several Horde skirmishes.","gear":"Substantial gold earned selling fish and cloth. Fishing 225 and Cooking 225 achieved; First Aid improved. No specific major gear upgrades recorded.","story":"Day Nine - A Fisherman's Fortune\n\nI awoke in Booty Bay to another warm morning in Stranglethorn Vale. After several days of dangerous expeditions, I had decided that today would be a quieter affair.\n\nMy first destination was Lake Nazferiti, where I spent the morning fishing beneath the jungle canopy. Later, I continued toward the mysterious waters of the Vile Reef.\n\nBy the end of my efforts, I had mastered Journeyman Fishing, reaching the limits of what my current training could teach me. If I wished to improve further, I would need to seek out an Expert.\n\nMy cooking had progressed just as well. After preparing countless catches, I had also mastered Journeyman Cooking. Once again, an Expert's instruction would be required before I could advance.\n\nTwo professions ready for the next stage of mastery. Not bad for a knight whose usual approach to wildlife involves a sword.\n\nThe day's efforts proved profitable as well. I sold fish and cloth through the Auction House, earning a rather satisfying amount of gold.\n\nI shall leave the exact sum unrecorded. Even a nobleman deserves some financial privacy.\n\nThe Cathedral of Light\n\nMy travels also brought me back to Stormwind, where I visited the magnificent Cathedral of Light to improve my First Aid skills.\n\nA knight's duty is not simply to defeat his enemies, but to protect those around him. Learning to treat wounds seemed a worthy pursuit.\n\nBesides, after witnessing some of my companions' approaches to combat, I suspect a good supply of bandages will prove invaluable.\n\nFriends, Foes, and Jungle Adventures\n\nLater, I reunited with Quillb Picklepocket, Spagetoll Cuh, and Thundrus Buccaklapper for adventures throughout Stranglethorn Vale.\n\nTogether, we hunted trolls, tigers, panthers, raptors, and hostile goblins while completing our quests.\n\nIt was mostly a relaxing afternoon among friends.\n\nMostly.\n\nOur travels brought us into contact with several members of the Horde, and before long, we found ourselves exchanging blows on more than one occasion.\n\nApparently, even a peaceful day in Stranglethorn requires the occasional demonstration of one's swordsmanship.\n\nFortunately, these skirmishes did little to spoil our spirits.\n\nAn Unexpected Friendship\n\nThe day also brought a new acquaintance: Psykopat Per, a male night elf rogue belonging to a guild called Dansk Folkeparty.\n\nThough he was not a member of the Plot Armor Crew, I was pleased to make his acquaintance.\n\nA knight's journey is shaped not only by the enemies he defeats, but also by the people he meets along the way.\n\nAnd I suspect a fellow with a name like Psykopat Per will be difficult to forget.\n\nBack to Booty Bay\n\nAs evening approached, I returned to the familiar harbor of Booty Bay.\n\nI had mastered Journeyman Fishing and Cooking, improved my First Aid, earned a handsome profit, completed quests alongside trusted companions, fought several Horde adventurers, and made a new friend.\n\nMy next challenge would be finding Expert trainers willing to help me take my skills further.\n\nThere had been no great dungeon or legendary treasure to claim.\n\nYet sometimes the finest adventures are those spent enjoying the simple pleasures of life.\n\nI retired for the evening content, ready for whatever tomorrow might bring.\n\nSer Atte Mardenholt\n\nThe Oath Outlives the Crown.","outcome":"A peaceful and profitable day, interrupted by some Horde encounters. Atte celebrated two profession milestones, strengthened his friendships and rested in Booty Bay, ready for the next adventure.","photos":[{"src":"assets/chapter-09-plate-01.webp","caption":"First aid training "},{"src":"assets/chapter-09-plate-02.webp","caption":"Cooking in STV"},{"src":"assets/chapter-09-plate-03.webp","caption":"Fishing in STV "}]},{"title":"Chapter X — Of Quiet Waters and Drowned Kingdoms","date":"2026-11-13","zone":"Stranglethorn Vale,The Drowned City, The Barrens, Razorfen Downs","companions":"Melan Holya, Thundrus Buccaklapper, Quillb Picklepocket, Spagetoll Cuh","quests":"Fishing at Atte's favourite spots in Stranglethorn Vale. Explored Razorfen Downs alongside Melan Holya. Later ventured into The Drowned City with Thundrus Buccaklapper, Melan Holya, Quillb Picklepocket, and Spagetoll Cuh.","gear":"No specific loot or equipment","story":"Day Ten — There are mornings when a man requires neither the ringing of steel nor the cries of battle. Only a fishing rod, a stretch of familiar shoreline, and the gentle promise that the next cast might prove more fortunate than the last.\n\nThus began my tenth day upon the road.\n\nOnce again, I returned to my favoured fishing grounds in Stranglethorn Vale. There is something strangely comforting about those waters. The jungle beyond may harbour every manner of beast, pirate, and bloodthirsty troll, yet along the shore, with a line cast into the sea, a man can almost pretend that Azeroth is a peaceful place.\n\nAlmost.\n\nFor even the most dedicated fisherman must eventually remember that he is, by profession, a warrior.\n\nAnd so I laid aside my fishing rod and returned to the considerably less peaceful business of adventuring.\nAmong the Dead of Razorfen Downs\nMy next undertaking brought me alongside Melan Holya, a priest whose company has become increasingly familiar upon these dangerous roads.\n\nTogether, we ventured into Razorfen Downs, deep within the southern Barrens.\n\nOnce a stronghold of the quilboar, the Downs had fallen beneath the shadow of the Scourge. Its thorn-walled passages and burial grounds bore the unmistakable presence of death, with the sinister influence of the lich Amnennar the Coldbringer hanging over the place.\n\nIt was a grim contrast to the waters of Stranglethorn.\n\nThere is little honour to be found in the work of the undead, and precious little comfort in a place where even the dead are denied their rest.\n\nYet such places cannot simply be abandoned to darkness.\n\nMelan and I pressed onward, answering the call of duty as best we could.\n\nA warrior's blade and a priest's faith make for fitting companions. One confronts the darkness with steel; the other reminds us why it must be confronted at all.\nBeneath the Waves — The Drowned City\nLater that day, another expedition called.\n\nThis time, a familiar company gathered:\n\nThundrus Buccaklapper, Melan Holya, Quillb Picklepocket, Spagetoll Cuh, and myself.\n\nOur destination was The Drowned City, the long-lost troll settlement of I'lalai, concealed beneath the waters off the coast of Stranglethorn Vale.\n\nHere, ancient troll mysteries and the unwelcome presence of the Saltseer Pirates had made a place already forgotten by the world more dangerous still.\n\nIt is one thing to face an enemy upon solid earth.\n\nIt is quite another to descend beneath the waves, where every breath becomes precious and the sea itself seems determined to keep its secrets.\n\nI could think of few better companions for such an undertaking.\n\nCheek brought the familiar resolve of a fellow warrior. Melan, the reassurance of the priesthood. Quillb, the talents of a rogue. And Spagetoll, those unsettling arts that warlocks insist are entirely under their control.\n\nI have learned not to ask too many questions of warlocks.\n\nSome answers are best left unknown.\n\nAnd so our company ventured into the mysteries of the drowned kingdom.\n\nI had begun the morning casting my line into the waters of Stranglethorn.\n\nBy evening, I found myself exploring the secrets concealed beneath them.\n\nA curious turn of events, even by the standards of my travels.\n\nPerhaps tomorrow I shall return to fishing.\n\nThough knowing my fortune, something beneath the surface will undoubtedly attempt to kill me.\n\nThe Oath Outlives the Crown. ⚔️","outcome":"Day 10 — began with a peaceful fishing session in Stranglethorn Vale and continued with two dungeon expeditions: Razorfen Downs with Melan Holya, followed by The Drowned City with Cheek, Quillb, Spagetoll, and Melan. No specific boss victories, loot drops, or further destinations have been recorded. Atte's journey continues, with familiar companions and new adventures still ahead.","photos":[{"src":"assets/editor-2026-11-21/chapter-10-plate-01.webp","caption":""},{"src":"assets/editor-2026-11-21/chapter-10-plate-02.webp","caption":""}]},{"title":"Chapter XI — Of Jungle Rains and Quiet Waters","date":"2026-11-14","zone":"Stranglethorn Vale · Booty Bay","companions":"","quests":"Continued questing throughout Stranglethorn Vale. Returned to fishing in the jungle waters despite the rain. Later rested on the wooden walkways of Booty Bay.","gear":"No significant loot, equipment upgrades, or new profession milestones recorded.","story":"Day eleven — I have begun to suspect that Stranglethorn Vale has two things in endless supply: rain and trouble.\n\nFortunately, it also has fish.\n\nMy eleventh day upon the road found me once again beneath the thick jungle canopy, continuing my duties throughout Stranglethorn Vale.\n\nThere is certainly no shortage of work in these lands. The paths wind through dense vegetation, the air hangs heavy with moisture, and one can scarcely travel far without being reminded that the jungle belongs more to its beasts than to any kingdom of men.\n\nNevertheless, I spent much of the day attending to quests and exploring the familiar surroundings.\n\nThe rain accompanied me, as it so often does.\nA Knight and His Fishing Rod\nEventually, I returned to an activity that has become something of a tradition upon my travels.\n\nFishing.\n\nI found a quiet stretch of water, cast my line, and allowed the troubles of Azeroth to concern themselves with someone else for a while.\n\nThere is a peculiar satisfaction in standing beside a jungle lake while the rain falls gently upon the surface.\n\nNo enemies to pursue. No dungeons to conquer. No companions attempting something spectacularly foolish.\n\nOnly the water, my fishing rod, and the occasional disappointment of an empty hook.\n\nI suspect my father never imagined that a son of House Mardenholt would become so devoted to catching fish.\n\nYet I maintain that patience is a virtue worthy of any knight.\n\nAnd a freshly cooked fish makes a considerably better supper than an enemy's severed head.\nShelter from the Storm\nLater, I found myself resting upon the wooden walkways of Booty Bay, surrounded by the familiar glow of torches and the steady patter of rain.\n\nFor a while, I simply sat and watched the storm.\n\nThere had been no grand battle worthy of a bard's finest song, nor some legendary treasure to carry home.\n\nOnly another day of honest adventuring, a little fishing, and a welcome moment of peace.\n\nBut I have come to appreciate such days.\n\nA wandering knight cannot spend every waking hour fighting monsters. Sometimes he must tend to simpler matters, enjoy the world around him, and remember that life contains more than duty and danger.\n\nAnd should tomorrow bring another dungeon, another perilous journey, or another impossible undertaking proposed by my companions, I shall be ready.\n\nPreferably after a little more fishing.\n\nSer Atte Mardenholt\n\nThe Oath Outlives the Crown.","outcome":"Another day of questing and fishing in Stranglethorn Vale came to a peaceful conclusion. Atte found time to rest in rainy Booty Bay, enjoying a welcome break from the dangers of the road.","photos":[{"src":"assets/chapter-11-plate-01.webp","caption":"Fishing STV "},{"src":"assets/chapter-11-plate-02.webp","caption":"Booty Bay "}]},{"title":"Chapter XII — Of Black Stallions and Scarlet Halls","date":"2026-11-15","zone":"Stranglethorn Vale · The Drowned City · Elwynn Forest · Eastvale Logging Camp · Wetlands · Menethil Harbor · Desolace · Scarlet Monastery Cathedral · Tirisfal Glades · Stormwind City","companions":"Matt Shamon","quests":"Quested alone in Stranglethorn Vale, hunting tigers, panthers, raptors, crocodiles and other wildlife. Completed The Drowned City with unnamed adventurers. Learned riding at Eastvale Logging Camp and acquired a Black Stallion in Menethil Harbor. Helped guildmate Matt Shamon with a Shaman totem quest in Desolace and flew above him on gryphons. Cleared Scarlet Monastery Cathedral with unnamed adventurers, then returned to Stormwind.","gear":"Learned riding skill and acquired first mount, a Black Stallion. No other significant equipment or loot recorded.","story":"Day Twelve — 15 November 2026\n\nI have spent a considerable portion of my life travelling the roads of Azeroth upon my own two feet.\n\nAcross forests and mountains, through ruined kingdoms and dangerous wilderness, I have walked farther than I care to remember.\n\nToday, at long last, I decided that my feet had suffered enough.\n\nBut I shall begin at the beginning.\n\nThe Beasts of Stranglethorn\n\nMy twelfth day upon the road began in the familiar jungles of Stranglethorn Vale.\n\nAfter yesterday's rain and fishing, I returned to the somewhat less peaceful business of completing quests.\n\nThe jungle remained as lively as ever.\n\nTigers prowled beneath the trees, panthers stalked through the undergrowth, and raptors continued their remarkable habit of regarding passing travellers as potential meals.\n\nEven the crocodiles seemed determined to make a nuisance of themselves.\n\nI spent the day hunting these creatures and attending to the tasks that had brought me into the region.\n\nThere was nothing particularly extraordinary about the work.\n\nNo grand discovery, no legendary treasure, and no tale of impossible odds overcome through heroic determination.\n\nOnly a warrior, his weapons, and an unreasonable number of dangerous animals.\n\nStill, honest work is honest work.\n\nAnd I suspect the people of Stranglethorn shall sleep more comfortably knowing that at least a few of its more disagreeable inhabitants have been dealt with.\n\nBeneath the Drowned City\n\nMy travels next carried me beneath the waters of Stranglethorn, into the mysterious depths of The Drowned City.\n\nI had ventured into that strange place before, alongside familiar members of the Plot Armor Crew.\n\nThis time, however, my companions were strangers.\n\nA collection of fellow adventurers whose names I did not come to know.\n\nThere is something curious about such expeditions.\n\nMen and women who have never met may find themselves standing shoulder to shoulder against dangers that would ordinarily demand the loyalty of old friends.\n\nFor a short while, their purposes become one.\n\nSurvive. Fight. Press onward.\n\nAnd, preferably, return to the surface.\n\nOur expedition proved successful.\n\nTogether, we completed the dungeon and emerged with another undertaking behind us.\n\nThere was no particularly remarkable treasure to remember the occasion by.\n\nYet I was satisfied.\n\nA knight need not know every companion's name to appreciate the value of fighting beside them.\n\nAnd I had more important business awaiting me.\n\nBusiness involving four legs.\n\nA Knight and His First Horse\n\nFor some time, I had considered acquiring a mount.\n\nI have never objected to walking. Indeed, there is much to admire about travelling on foot. One sees the countryside, learns its roads, and occasionally discovers places that might otherwise have passed unnoticed.\n\nUnfortunately, one also discovers precisely how enormous Azeroth truly is.\n\nAnd I had grown rather tired of making that discovery.\n\nThus, I travelled to Eastvale Logging Camp, in the peaceful forests of Elwynn, where I finally learned the skill of riding.\n\nIt was a significant occasion.\n\nFor a knight of House Mardenholt, whose ancestors had served the lands of Lordaeron, the companionship of a faithful horse seemed a fitting part of the life I had chosen.\n\nYet learning to ride was only half the undertaking.\n\nI still required a horse.\n\nAnd for that, my journey carried me north to Menethil Harbor, among the marshes of the Wetlands.\n\nThere, at long last, I acquired my very first mount.\n\nA magnificent Black Stallion.\n\nI confess that few acquisitions upon my travels have pleased me quite so much.\n\nI have obtained weapons, armor, and strange enchanted objects whose purposes would trouble the minds of wiser men than myself.\n\nBut a horse?\n\nA horse is a treasure of a different kind.\n\nA companion for the roads ahead.\n\nA means of reaching distant places without wearing my boots into oblivion.\n\nAnd perhaps, most importantly, a sign that my journey had entered a new chapter.\n\nI thought of the knights of Lordaeron, riding beneath the banners of their houses, and of the traditions my family had endeavoured to preserve.\n\nHouse Mardenholt possesses neither the wealth nor the influence it once knew.\n\nYet its name endures.\n\nAnd now, upon a black stallion of my own, I felt a little more like the knight I had always aspired to become.\n\nI believe my father would have approved.\n\nThough I suspect he might have questioned why it had taken me quite so long.\n\nA Friend Among the Wastes of Desolace\n\nMy travels also carried me once more to the barren lands of Desolace.\n\nThere, I helped a fellow member of the Plot Armor Crew: Matt Shamon, a dwarven shaman with a totem quest to attend to.\n\nDesolace is hardly the most welcoming place in Azeroth.\n\nIts dry earth, rugged hills, and unforgiving wilderness stand in sharp contrast to the lush jungles of Stranglethorn.\n\nYet even in such a desolate country, there is satisfaction to be found in good company.\n\nA knight's duties, after all, are not limited to his own quests and ambitions.\n\nSometimes the greatest service one may offer is simply to stand beside a friend when assistance is needed.\n\nThere was also a rather amusing moment during our travels.\n\nWe took to the skies upon gryphons, and I found myself flying above Matt as we passed over the rugged country below.\n\nI must admit, there is something thoroughly enjoyable about watching the world pass beneath one's feet instead of enduring every mile upon them.\n\nAnd seeing a fellow adventurer flying below makes the experience all the more entertaining.\n\nI suspect Matt may have had a somewhat different perspective on the arrangement.\n\nNevertheless, I was pleased to have helped him with his totem quest.\n\nFor the strength of the Plot Armor Crew lies not merely in the dungeons we conquer together, but in the willingness of its members to help one another along the road.\n\nThe Scarlet Cathedral\n\nMy day's adventures were not yet finished.\n\nWith my newly acquired mount and a renewed enthusiasm for travelling, I turned my attention toward another familiar stronghold.\n\nThe Scarlet Monastery.\n\nI had previously explored its Graveyard and Library, witnessing the troubling zealotry that had consumed the once-hallowed institution.\n\nThis time, my destination was the Cathedral.\n\nOnce again, I found myself travelling alongside adventurers whose names I did not know.\n\nAnd once again, we had a common purpose.\n\nThe Scarlet Crusade had transformed a place of faith into a fortress of fanaticism.\n\nIt is a troubling thing to witness.\n\nI have always held that faith should inspire compassion, courage, and the protection of those who cannot defend themselves.\n\nBut the Scarlet Crusade had allowed fear and hatred to twist those virtues into something far darker.\n\nA man may claim to fight in the name of righteousness.\n\nThat does not make his cause righteous.\n\nTogether, our company pressed through the Cathedral and successfully completed the expedition.\n\nAnother dungeon conquered.\n\nAnother chapter in my travels brought to a satisfying conclusion.\n\nAnd although there was no remarkable treasure to carry away, I considered the undertaking worthwhile.\n\nThe day's greatest reward was already mine.\n\nMy horse.\n\nThe Road Back to Stormwind\n\nWith the Cathedral behind me, I made my way back toward Stormwind.\n\nIt had been a productive day.\n\nI had hunted the beasts of Stranglethorn Vale, returned to the mysterious Drowned City, travelled across the Eastern Kingdoms to learn the art of riding, and acquired my very first horse.\n\nI had helped Matt with his shaman's totem quest in Desolace and shared a gryphon flight with a fellow member of the Plot Armor Crew.\n\nThen, apparently deciding that such accomplishments were insufficient, I had ventured into the Scarlet Monastery and completed its Cathedral.\n\nNot a poor day's work.\n\nYet as I returned to the great city, my thoughts were less concerned with the enemies I had defeated than with the journeys still awaiting me.\n\nTomorrow, I intended to make my way toward Riverglades.\n\nA new destination.\n\nAnother road.\n\nAnd this time, I would not be travelling upon my own two feet.\n\nI must confess, that thought pleased me enormously.\n\nPerhaps the finest reward of this entire day was not the completion of two dungeons, nor the satisfaction of duties fulfilled.\n\nPerhaps it was simply knowing that the next great adventure would begin with the sound of hooves upon the road.\n\nI returned to Stormwind content.\n\nMy sword remained ready.\n\nMy oath remained unbroken.\n\nAnd for the first time upon these travels, I had a horse to carry me toward whatever awaited beyond the horizon.\n\nSer Atte Mardenholt\n\nThe Oath Outlives the Crown.","outcome":"Day Twelve: Atte completed The Drowned City and Scarlet Monastery Cathedral, learned riding, acquired his first Black Stallion, and helped guildmate Matt Shamon with his totem quest in Desolace. Returned to Stormwind intending to travel to Riverglades on Day XIII.","photos":[{"src":"assets/editor-2026-11-21/chapter-12-plate-01.webp","caption":"Matt Shamon and me on the way to Decolace. "},{"src":"assets/editor-2026-11-21/chapter-12-plate-02.webp","caption":"Black stailon"}]},{"title":"Chapter XIII — Of New Horizons and the Smith's Craft","date":"2026-11-16","zone":"Riverglades · Farholdekeep","companions":"","quests":"Explored and quested alone in Riverglades, including Farholdekeep. May have fought murlocs (uncertain recollection). Improved Blacksmithing to 150.","gear":"Blacksmithing skill reached 150. No notable crafted item, loot or equipment upgrades recorded.","story":"Day Thirteen — 16 November 2026\n\nThere is a particular pleasure in setting forth toward a place one has never seen before.\n\nNo familiar roads to follow, no certainty of what might lie beyond the next bend, and no knowledge of whether the inhabitants will greet a traveller with kindness or attempt to separate him from his belongings.\n\nOr his head.\n\nAfter yesterday's adventures, and the rather splendid acquisition of my first horse, I felt ready to discover somewhere new.\n\nAnd so my journey brought me to Riverglades.\n\nInto Unfamiliar Lands\n\nI have travelled through many corners of Azeroth since leaving the lands of my youth.\n\nFrom the forests of Elwynn to the jungles of Stranglethorn, from the cold mountains of Dun Morogh to the troubled remains of Lordaeron, the world has shown me both remarkable beauty and considerable danger.\n\nYet there are always new places waiting to be discovered.\n\nRiverglades was such a place.\n\nI spent the day exploring the region, becoming acquainted with its surroundings and attending to the quests that awaited me.\n\nFor once, there were no familiar voices accompanying me along the road.\n\nNo Quillb with his rogue's cunning.\n\nNo Thundrus with his warrior's determination.\n\nAnd no priest to remind me that charging headlong into danger is not always the wisest course of action.\n\nI was entirely on my own.\n\nNot that I minded.\n\nThere is a certain satisfaction in travelling alone, when a man may choose his own direction and enjoy the simple freedom of following wherever the road leads.\n\nAlthough I confess that the company of good friends makes even the most disagreeable wilderness rather more entertaining.\n\nTrouble Among the Waters\n\nMy work throughout Riverglades brought its share of ordinary dangers.\n\nIf memory serves, I believe there were murlocs among the creatures I encountered.\n\nA curious people, the murlocs.\n\nI have faced undead horrors, savage beasts, dangerous warlords, and creatures whose origins would trouble the minds of learned scholars.\n\nYet few possess quite the same talent for producing noises that sound like someone attempting to gargle an entire river.\n\nI cannot claim to remember every creature I fought that day, nor every task I completed.\n\nBut I continued my exploration and carried out the work before me.\n\nThere were no legendary battles to recount.\n\nNo grand victories demanding the attention of minstrels.\n\nOnly another day's honest adventuring.\n\nAnd sometimes that is enough.\n\nThe Smith's Craft\n\nYet the day held one accomplishment of which I was particularly pleased.\n\nMy Blacksmithing had finally reached 150.\n\nEver since taking up the hammer and learning the principles of working metal, I have found considerable satisfaction in the craft.\n\nA sword may win a battle, but it is the smith who gives that sword its form.\n\nArmor may preserve a warrior's life, but before it can withstand a blow, someone must first possess the patience and skill to shape it.\n\nThere is honour in such work.\n\nI have long admired the craftsmanship of those who dedicate their lives to the forge, and I take some pride in knowing that my own abilities continue to improve.\n\nI had not produced some magnificent weapon destined to become a family heirloom.\n\nNor had I fashioned armor worthy of the greatest knights of Stormwind.\n\nBut I had reached another milestone.\n\nOne hundred and fifty.\n\nA respectable achievement for a man who seems to spend most of his days wandering into dangerous places.\n\nI suspect that, given enough time, I may yet become a reasonably competent blacksmith.\n\nProvided, of course, that Azeroth allows me to remain near an anvil long enough.\n\nA Quiet Evening in Riverglades\n\nAs the day drew to its conclusion, I remained in Riverglades.\n\nThere had been no great dungeon expedition, no remarkable treasure, and no company of familiar friends gathered around me.\n\nBut I had explored new lands, continued my duties, and improved a craft that I had come to value.\n\nI considered that a satisfactory day's work.\n\nA knight's journey cannot consist entirely of great battles and legendary deeds.\n\nSome days are remembered for the enemies defeated.\n\nOthers for the friendships formed.\n\nAnd occasionally, a day is worth remembering simply because one has learned something new.\n\nTomorrow would undoubtedly bring further adventures.\n\nWhere the road might lead, I could not yet say.\n\nBut I would be ready to follow it.\n\nWith a sword at my side, a horse to carry me, and an ever-improving understanding of the blacksmith's hammer.\n\nNot a poor collection of accomplishments, if I may say so myself.\n\nSer Atte Mardenholt\n\nThe Oath Outlives the Crown.","outcome":"Atte explored Riverglades and Farholdekeep alone, reaching Blacksmithing 150. He remained in Riverglades at the end of the day; the next destination was not recorded.","photos":[{"src":"assets/editor-2026-11-21/chapter-13-plate-01.webp","caption":"Farhold Keep "},{"src":"assets/editor-2026-11-21/chapter-13-plate-02.webp","caption":"Farhold Keep"}]},{"title":"Chapter XIV — Of Thornbound Shadows and Four Unlikely Fish","date":"2026-11-17","zone":"Razorfen Downs · The Barrens · Feralas · Desolace · Swamp of Sorrows · Stranglethorn Vale","companions":"Zelica Lockhart","quests":"Completed Razorfen Downs. Recruited paladin healer Zelica Lockhart to the party and the Plot Armor Crew. Caught Feralas Ahi, Sar'theris Striker, Misty Reed Mahi Mahi, and Savage Coast Blue Sailfin; returned all four fish to Nat Pagle and completed his quest.","gear":"Learned Artisan Fishing after returning the four quest fish to Nat Pagle. No other gear milestones recorded.","outcome":"Razorfen Downs cleared; Zelica Lockhart joined the guild; Nat Pagle's fishing quest completed; Artisan Fishing learned.","story":"Day Fourteen — 17 November 2026\n\nI have come to accept that the life of a wandering knight is seldom predictable.\n\nOne day, a man may find himself standing against the horrors that dwell beneath the earth. The next, he may be travelling across half of Azeroth in pursuit of a fish.\n\nOn this particular day, I managed both.\n\nI am beginning to suspect that my ancestors would find my adventures rather difficult to explain.\n\nNevertheless, the road continues, and so must I.\n\nBeneath the Thorns of Razorfen Downs\n\nMy fourteenth day brought me once more to Razorfen Downs, that grim and troubled place hidden within the southern reaches of the Barrens.\n\nI had ventured into its depths before, and I knew well enough that it was no place for the careless.\n\nThe ancient thorn-covered passages and burial grounds have long since fallen beneath a darker influence. Where the quilboar once honoured their dead, the shadow of undeath has made a mockery of their resting places.\n\nIt is a miserable thing to witness.\n\nDeath should bring peace, not servitude.\n\nYet there are those in Azeroth who seem determined to deny even that small mercy.\n\nAnd so, once again, I took up my sword and ventured beneath the thorns.\n\nThis expedition, however, brought a new companion into our company.\n\nZelica Lockhart.\n\nA paladin, and one who had devoted her abilities to the healing of others.\n\nThere is something reassuring about the presence of a servant of the Light when one must venture into places where darkness has made its home.\n\nA warrior may possess a strong sword arm and a sturdy suit of armour, but neither makes him invincible.\n\nI have learned that lesson often enough.\n\nAnd while I take considerable pride in my ability to stand against an enemy, I have never been foolish enough to believe that courage alone can mend a wound.\n\nZelica joined our party as its healer, bringing the skills of her calling to an undertaking that demanded cooperation from all involved.\n\nTogether, our company pressed through Razorfen Downs.\n\nThe expedition proved successful.\n\nAnother descent into that grim place had been completed, and we emerged with the dungeon behind us.\n\nYet the day brought something more lasting than another victory.\n\nZelica Lockhart joined the Plot Armor Crew.\n\nI have come to believe that a guild is measured not merely by the strength of its warriors, but by the company they choose to keep.\n\nA good healer is always welcome.\n\nA good companion, more so.\n\nAnd I was pleased to see another name added to the growing fellowship with whom I share these roads.\n\nMay the Light guide her steps.\n\nFor if our company continues to wander into places such as Razorfen Downs, I suspect she shall have no shortage of work.\n\nThe Fisherman's Challenge\n\nHaving spent part of the day amid the dead, I turned my attention toward a considerably more peaceful pursuit.\n\nFishing.\n\nOr so I believed.\n\nFor this particular undertaking began with Nat Pagle, a fisherman whose understanding of a reasonable day's work differs rather dramatically from my own.\n\nNat had a challenge for me.\n\nFour rare fish.\n\nFour distant locations.\n\nAnd, apparently, an expectation that I should think nothing of travelling across Azeroth to retrieve them.\n\nI have accepted quests involving dangerous beasts, hostile armies, forgotten ruins, and creatures that should never have been disturbed.\n\nYet I confess that few assignments have sounded quite so absurd as this one.\n\nNevertheless, I had set my mind upon improving my fishing, and a knight of House Mardenholt does not abandon a task merely because it requires an unreasonable amount of travelling.\n\nEven when that task involves fish.\n\nAnd so my peculiar expedition began.\n\nFour Waters, Four Catches\n\nAmong my quarry was the Feralas Ahi, found in the waters of the Verdantis River in Feralas.\n\nThere is something remarkable about that country. Its great forests and abundant greenery stand in striking contrast to the harsher regions through which I have travelled.\n\nYet I had not come to admire the landscape alone.\n\nI had come for a fish.\n\nAnd a fish I caught.\n\nMy task also carried me to the desolate shores of Desolace, where the Sar'theris Strand held another of Nat's requested specimens.\n\nThe Sar'theris Striker.\n\nA curious name for a fish, though I have encountered stranger things upon my travels.\n\nDesolace is hardly the first place I would recommend for a pleasant afternoon beside the water.\n\nNevertheless, the catch was made.\n\nThen there was the Misty Reed Mahi Mahi, found along the Misty Reed Strand in the Swamp of Sorrows.\n\nOf all the places in Azeroth where a man might choose to enjoy a quiet fishing expedition, the Swamp of Sorrows would not rank particularly highly upon my list.\n\nIts gloomy wetlands are not exactly inviting.\n\nBut I was not there for comfort.\n\nAnd before my work was finished, another rare fish had been added to my collection.\n\nFinally, there was the Savage Coast Blue Sailfin, dwelling along the coast of Stranglethorn Vale.\n\nStranglethorn.\n\nA place I have come to know rather well.\n\nI have hunted its beasts, walked its jungle paths, endured its seemingly endless rain, and spent more hours fishing its waters than I care to admit.\n\nIt seemed only fitting that one of Nat's rare specimens should be found there.\n\nAnd so the fourth fish was caught.\n\nMy collection was complete.\n\nFour fish from four distant corners of Azeroth.\n\nA rather extraordinary accomplishment, considering that not one of them had required me to draw my sword.\n\nI suspect there is a lesson in that somewhere.\n\nThough I am not entirely certain what it is.\n\nThe Making of an Artisan Fisherman\n\nWith all four rare fish secured, there remained one final matter.\n\nI had to return to Nat Pagle.\n\nAfter travelling through forests, desolate coastlines, gloomy swamps, and tropical jungles, I was determined that this peculiar undertaking should not end with four fish simply occupying space among my belongings.\n\nAnd so I made my way back to the fisherman who had sent me upon this extraordinary errand.\n\nAll four specimens were delivered.\n\nThe challenge was complete.\n\nAnd at last, Nat Pagle taught me the art of Artisan Fishing.\n\nI must confess that I felt a considerable measure of pride.\n\nWhen I first took up a fishing rod, I regarded the activity as little more than a pleasant distraction from the hardships of adventuring.\n\nA quiet moment beside the water.\n\nA chance to gather something useful for supper.\n\nPerhaps even a welcome excuse to avoid being struck by particularly unpleasant creatures for an hour or two.\n\nYet somewhere along the road, that simple pastime had become something more.\n\nI had devoted time, patience, and no small amount of determination to improving my abilities.\n\nAnd now I had earned the right to call myself an Artisan Fisherman.\n\nA knight of Lordaeron.\n\nA warrior of the Alliance.\n\nA blacksmith of respectable ability.\n\nAnd now, apparently, a rather accomplished fisherman.\n\nI wonder what my ancestors would make of such a collection of titles.\n\nI suspect the fishing would raise the greatest number of questions.\n\nNevertheless, I consider it an honourable achievement.\n\nFor mastery is not granted freely, whether one holds a sword, a hammer, or a fishing rod.\n\nIt must be earned.\n\nAnd today, I had earned it.\n\nA Knight's Unusual Duties\n\nAs I considered the day's undertakings, I could not help but find some amusement in them.\n\nI had ventured into the darkness of Razorfen Downs, fought alongside a new companion, and welcomed a paladin into the ranks of the Plot Armor Crew.\n\nThen I had travelled across Azeroth to gather four exceedingly particular fish for a man whose enthusiasm for fishing has evidently become everyone else's problem.\n\nAnd, having returned with my catches, I had become an Artisan Fisherman.\n\nSuch is the strange nature of my travels.\n\nWhen I first set forth beneath the name of House Mardenholt, I imagined that my days would be filled with noble duties, honourable battles, and the defence of those unable to defend themselves.\n\nAnd indeed, those responsibilities remain close to my heart.\n\nBut the road has taught me that there is more to life than the clash of steel.\n\nThere is fellowship.\n\nThere is patience.\n\nThere is the satisfaction of mastering a craft.\n\nAnd, occasionally, there is the peculiar triumph of travelling across half the known world to impress a fisherman.\n\nI believe my father would have approved of my dedication.\n\nThough I suspect he might have raised an eyebrow at the considerable distance travelled in pursuit of four fish.\n\nStill, I consider the day a success.\n\nAnother dungeon completed.\n\nAnother companion welcomed.\n\nFour rare catches delivered.\n\nAnd a new mastery of the fisherman's craft.\n\nNot a poor day's work for a wandering knight.\n\nTomorrow, perhaps, I shall return to the more conventional business of fighting monsters.\n\nThough knowing my fortune, someone will undoubtedly ask me to catch another fish.\n\nSer Atte Mardenholt\n\nThe Oath Outlives the Crown.","photos":[],"inlinePhotos":[],"headings":["Beneath the Thorns of Razorfen Downs","The Fisherman's Challenge","Four Waters, Four Catches","The Making of an Artisan Fisherman","A Knight's Unusual Duties"]}];
